  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1020 PM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nA slug of moisture will meet up with an upper trough and will \nbring breezy to very breezy conditions and precipitation chances \nSunday. Anomalous moisture will still be present across the area \nwith PWs of around 0.9\"-1.1\". Plenty of shear will be at play as \nwell (0-6km shear of 50-70kt). Storm chances begin in the Gila \nregion and western areas bordering AZ in the late afternoon \n(~3/4PM) and will push eastward through the evening hours Sunday. \nMost recent CAMs show a line of storms initially as it gets into \nthe CWA but then struggles as it nears the Rio Grande Valley. This\nwould make sense as this trough and it's associated pacific front\npushes towards El Paso, the sun sets and capping will start to \nbuild up and may just transition to more showery activity after \ndark. CAPE is the most uncertain in this forecast. Models continue\nto suggest afternoon CAPEs will be best in far west TX and S. \nOtero Co. with lower CAPEs out west where the front will reach \nfirst. \n\nThe SPC has placed portions of our area in a Marginal risk for \nsevere storms Sunday with a focus for damaging wind gusts from \nstorms. In addition, the Gila region and portions of northern \nHidalgo Co. are in a Slight risk (Level 2/4) for excessive rainfall \nwith storms. A Marginal risk (Level 1/4) covers the Sacramento Mtns, \nNM Bootheel, Sierra Co. and portions of Luna Co. The Gila region, \nBlack Range, and northern Hidalgo are in a Flood Watch currently. \nStorms will be moving fast but there will be concern for training \nstorms causing flash flooding. \n\nStorms exit Monday morning in the earlier morning hours, around \nsunrise. Lingering showers possible in the remaining morning hours \nbut much drier air will push in right behind the front leaving a \nnice and dry day across the area on Monday but will be a bit breezy \nlike Sunday will be. This nice, quiet, and dry weather will persist \nthrough the work week.",
